      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the RMA seems too disruptive, you're right to look for other diagnostic paths before committing to a full swap. You could try monitoring the link using specialized tools that capture raw physical layer data, if your platform allows for it. Sometimes, these brief bursts are indeed caused by rhythmic electrical noise—like a nearby motor cycling on or off, or perhaps some other piece of equipment powering up and causing a momentary voltage dip. This kind of environmental noise is often the culprit, even if the cable itself looks fine.",
